--TEST--
`Core\Config`'s four members on a host with no configuration file at all — `rule:config/ini-set-is-core-config-set`, `rule:config/the-root-is-config-else-nvs-toml-else-the-shipped-defaults` step 3, `rule:config/three-changeability-classes`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A case runs in a working directory of its own with no `nvs.toml` in it, so
// this is `rule:config/the-root-is-config-else-nvs-toml-else-the-shipped-defaults` step 3's host: the shipped defaults, which are a
// complete and valid configuration and not a failure to find one. Every member
// of `rule:config/ini-set-is-core-config-set`'s API has to have an answer on it, and none of them throws —
// there is nothing in this file to `catch`.

// Nothing states `[limits] memory`, so nothing is in force for it.
echo "get=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";

// `rule:config/three-changeability-classes`: `[limits]` states the default and `[limits.hard]` the ceiling. With
// neither stated there is no ceiling to exceed, so a `Runtime` directive moves
// freely — the same rule that stops the set at `512M` on a host whose operator
// wrote one.
if (Core\Config::set("memory", "512M")) {
    echo "set=ok", "\n";
} else {
    echo "set=refused", "\n";
}
echo "after=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";

// A `System` directive is refused whatever the tree says, because the class
// answers *who may set it* before any ceiling is consulted.
if (Core\Config::set("cache.dir", "/tmp/nvs")) {
    echo "system=ok", "\n";
} else {
    echo "system=refused", "\n";
}

// `restore` puts the file's own value back — here, nothing.
Core\Config::restore("memory");
echo "restored=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";

// And `all` is what the request can see: the overlay it has just emptied, over
// a tree with nothing in it.
echo "all=", Core\Arr::count(Core\Config::all()), "\n";
--EXPECT--
get=unset
set=ok
after=512M
system=refused
restored=unset
all=0
